{# Acknowledgement letter template — CRA Article 14 §6.
   Rendered by the ack_to_reporter step of playbook.cra_cvd@v1.

   Fields:
     case_id            : operator-assigned CVD case identifier.
     reporter_display   : the reporter's chosen display name, or
                          "the reporter" when anonymous / pseudonymous.
     ack_timestamp_iso  : ISO 8601 timestamp the receipt was stamped at.
     operator_display   : operator name as published in the CVD policy
                          / security.txt Contact line.
     cvd_policy_url     : public URL of the operator's CVD policy so
                          the reporter can cite the same source the
                          acknowledgement stamps against.
     next_update_after  : ISO 8601 date the reporter can expect the
                          next status update (triage verdict or a
                          rationale under the policy window).
     support_pgp_fpr    : PGP key fingerprint used to sign this letter.
                          Empty when the reporter has not requested a
                          signed acknowledgement.

   The rendered acknowledgement is delivered by the PGP-signed delivery
   adapter (sibling adapter card) and persisted on the case ledger under
   __reporter_ack_ts__.

   CRA Article 14 §6 requires the manufacturer to acknowledge a received
   vulnerability report within the window declared in the operator's CVD
   policy; the SecOps-NG operator baseline is 3 working days.
#}
Subject: [{{ operator_display }}] Vulnerability disclosure received — case {{ case_id }}

Hello {{ reporter_display }},

Thank you for reaching out. We have received your vulnerability
disclosure and opened case {{ case_id }} at {{ ack_timestamp_iso }}.

This message is the receipt required by our coordinated vulnerability
disclosure policy at {{ cvd_policy_url }} — it stamps the case with a
durable acknowledgement timestamp so both parties have a citable point
of reference for the disclosure window.

What happens next:

  1. Our triage team reviews the report and either reproduces the
     vulnerability or writes back with a rationale within the policy
     window.
  2. We will send you a status update no later than {{ next_update_after }},
     even if triage is still in progress at that date.
  3. If the report is in scope and we develop a fix, we will coordinate
     a public disclosure date with you before publishing the advisory.
     We will ask up front whether you would like to be credited by name
     and whether you would like to review the advisory text before it
     goes public.

Please keep this case identifier ({{ case_id }}) on any follow-up so we
can join the correspondence back to the same case.

Kind regards,
{{ operator_display }} — vulnerability disclosure

{% if support_pgp_fpr -%}
--
Signed with PGP fingerprint {{ support_pgp_fpr }}.
The public key is published at {{ cvd_policy_url }}.
{%- endif %}
